      id: "explanation-27", type: "explanation", variant: "simple",
      content: "TRANSPARENCY: RTI. In 1997 a working group under H.D. Shourie drafted what became the Freedom of Information Act, 2002 — enacted but never notified, widely called a feeble law. Tamil Nadu had already become the first state with its own RTI law in 1997. The RTI Act, 2005 was drafted not by bureaucrats but by the National Advisory Council. A sound RTI regime needs four things: wide access (minimal fees, free for BPL), penalties for denial (Rs 250/day up to Rs 25,000), independent appeal (CIC and SICs), and maximum disclosure with minimal exemption. RTI's philosophy balances five values: transparency, accountability, democracy, confidentiality and efficiency.\n\nThe Act is wholly RETROSPECTIVE — it applies to information held by public authorities regardless of when it was created. Section 8 lists the exemptions; almost all exempted information must be released after 20 years, except Cabinet decisions, breach of legislature privilege, and anything prejudicing sovereignty/security/strategic/economic interest or foreign relations. Infringement of copyright is the ONLY absolute exemption. The Act overrides the Official Secrets Act, 1923. Intelligence and security agencies in the Second Schedule are exempt — but not for corruption and human rights violations.\n\nThe CIC has one Chief Information Commissioner and up to ten Information Commissioners appointed by the President on the advice of a committee of the PM (chair), Leader of Opposition (Lok Sabha) and a Union Cabinet Minister nominated by the PM. The RTI Amendment Act, 2019 removed the fixed five-year tenure and the salary parity with the CEC/ECs, handing both to the Central Government by rules — currently 3 years or age 65. Critics call this executive overreach; the government calls it administrative flexibility. Either way the CIC is a STATUTORY, not constitutional, body.\n\nCORRUPTION: Transparency International defines it as 'private abuse of public office' and advocates the 'Elephant Test' — hard to define, easy to point at. Types worth memorising: black (universally condemned) / white (tolerated, e.g. speed money) / grey (contested); grass-eating (won't refuse a bribe) vs meat-eating (actively hunts); coercive (citizen bribed to get their due) vs collusive (both sides gain at public cost); retail/petty vs policy/grand (state capture, crony capitalism).\n\nINSTITUTIONS: The CBI began as the Special Police Establishment (1941), got statutory backing through the Delhi Special Police Establishment Act, 1946, and acquired its present name in 1963. It has NO suo-motu jurisdiction in states — it needs state consent. Vineet Narain (1997) gave the CBI Director a minimum two-year tenure, put CBI's anti-corruption work under CVC superintendence, mandated a committee-based appointment, and struck down the Single Directive (which had required prior sanction to even investigate joint-secretary-and-above officers). The Single Directive returned as Section 6A of the DSPE Act, was struck down again in 2014 for violating equality, and returned once more as Section 17A of the PCA in 2018 — this time requiring prior approval for ALL public servants, except where caught red-handed.\n\nThe CVC was created as an EXECUTIVE body in 1964 on the recommendation of the K. Santhanam Committee (1962) and became a STATUTORY body only through the CVC Act, 2003. It has a Central Vigilance Commissioner plus not more than two Vigilance Commissioners; term 4 years or age 65; selection by PM, Home Minister and Leader of Opposition; removal only for proved misbehaviour or incapacity on a reference to the Supreme Court; salary from the Consolidated Fund. In PJ Thomas (2011) the SC held selection must rest on CONSENSUS of all members, not the government's built-in majority.\n\nACCOUNTABILITY FROM BELOW: Social audit means the beneficiaries themselves examine a scheme's finances and records in a public hearing — jan sunwai. It originated with the MKSS jan sunwais in 1990s Rajasthan, whose movement produced the RTI Act itself. It is a statutory requirement under very few programmes — MGNREGA being the main one. Andhra Pradesh's SSAAT and Jharkhand's SAU are the leading institutional models; the SC praised social audits in Swaraj Abhiyan (2016); Meghalaya's 2017 Act is the model social audit law.\n\nOMBUDSMAN: The institution began in Sweden in 1809. The First ARC proposed a two-tier Lokpal-Lokayukta design. India's first Lokpal bill came in 1968; the Lokpal Act passed in 2013; the Lokpal was finally constituted in 2019 with Justice P.C. Ghosh as first chairperson. Maharashtra was the first state to appoint a Lokayukta (1971).",
